/*Functions are generally declared as

function_name :: (input_0 : input_0_type, input_1 : input_1_type...) -> return_type

Functions can have 0..n inputs and 0..1 outputs.

- ctrl+alt+d toggles debug mode
- ctrl+alt+c compiles
- ctrl+shift+g to move between code and asm lines
- more function info below the main function
*/
//Add function has 2 inputs and 1 output
Add :: (i0 : int, i1 : int) -> int {
	ri : int = i0 + i1;
	return ri;
}

//Update function has 1 input and 0 outputs
Update :: (i : *int) {
	i.* += 1;
}

//Chord function has 0 inputs and 1 output
Chord :: () -> [3] float {
	return float.[0.5,3.5,7.5];	
}

//The main function has no inputs and no outputs.
main :: () {
	/*
	- set a breakpoint with mouse click on left grey column
	or f9 at text cursor
	- f5 to run/debug
	- f11 will step into the function during debugging
	*/
	i : int = Add(1,1);
	Update(*i);

	c : [3] float = Chord();
}

/*A function call is 
- make space for return values on stack (no instructions)
- push all registers onto the stack
- move all inputs after the next sbp reg address
- update the sbp reg to new function call stack
- set the return instruction index
- call/jmp to the function instruction index
- pop registers from the stack after returning



- Explicit Add function call notes:
push_all_stack_reg [sbp+8]
	- [sbp+0] is the return int value
mov [sbp+160], 1, 8;
	- [sbp+160] in main is [sbp+0] in Add
mov [sbp+168], 1, 8;
	- [sbp+168] in main is [sbp+8] in Add
add sbp, sbp, 160;
	- update sbp for the function call
mov [sbp-8], 14;
	- the return asm index relative to Add function sbp
call 0;
	- equivalent to jmp to asm index 0
	- Add function starts on asm index 0
pop_all_stack_reg [srp-152];
	- srp is set to the sbp of Add function in the ret instruction
	- sbp is updated before returning because when returning
	we want to see the correct watch values otherwise 
	the watch values would be based on the sbp before popping
	- all registers could be popped in ret instruction
	but then we would not have an instruction to come back to
	when returning from the function being called
	- see ret execution code in thegame_code.jai, function ExecMInstr

- because we set return values on the previous stack
we can copy the return value directly in memory
from the function call at the return operator
- see KEYWORD_RETURN and CompileFunctionCall in thegame_code.jai
*/